/** The body of skills/executor/SKILL.md, without its frontmatter. MCP servers send it as their instructions. */
export const executorIntro = "# Executor\n\n## What Executor is\n\nExecutor is a host that runs apps: TypeScript packages that expose tools,\nstorage, UI, accounts and background work. Agents reach every app through the\nMCP tools `skills`, `search`, `describe`, `call` and `execute`, with each app's\nslug as its namespace. Credentials go only through Executor's secure connection\nlinks, never through chat or source code. Some calls pause for user approval.\n\nThis is the Executor app's `executor` skill. Read its other skills with\n`skills({app: \"executor\", name: \"code-mode\"})`; `skills({})` lists them all.\n\n## Find and call tools\n\n`search({ query: \"...\" })` returns each tool's exact `path`, such as\n`tools.<slug>.<tool>`, and input type; `describe({ paths })` returns full\nsignatures. Never guess argument names.\n\nFor a few independent calls, use `call({ calls: [{ path, input }] })`; `calls`\nis always an array and runs concurrently. Results come back as an array in the\nsame order, each with `ok` and its `value` or `error`. Pass a large input,\nsuch as a whole page or document, through `call` as it is: an `execute`\nprogram, every string in it included, stops at 65,536 characters, and `call`\nhas no such limit. When the input is a file and the tool offers its own\nupload step, such as an upload URL, use that instead, sending the file from\nyour own code; `execute` cannot reach the network. Never shrink, split or hash\na value to fit a program.\n\nWrite a JavaScript program for `execute` when one call's result feeds another,\nor a large result should be filtered before it reaches you. Inside it, the same\npaths are async functions, and `tools.search` and `tools.search.describe` work\nas above. Return only the data you need. For depth, read the `code-mode` skill.\nWhen `call` or `execute` pauses for approval or input, show the request to the\nuser and resume it; never repeat the call or run the program again.\n\n## See images, hear audio and read files\n\nYou can look at any image, listen to any audio and read any file a tool gives\nyou. Wherever the value `call` or `execute` returns holds\n`{ type: \"image\", mimeType, data }`, `{ type: \"audio\", mimeType, data }` or a\nfile as `{ type: \"resource\", resource: { uri, mimeType, blob } }`, with the\nbytes in standard or URL-safe base64, you receive that block as media or a\nfile, such as a PDF, and the JSON shows it without its bytes plus\n`contentIndex`. Screenshots from MCP servers already arrive in that shape. When\na tool returns the bytes some other way, such as an email attachment as\n`{ base64 }`, return them in that shape from `execute`: an image block for an\nimage, and otherwise a resource whose `uri` ends in the file's name:\n\n```js\nconst file = await tools.gmail.attachment({ messageId, attachmentId });\nreturn {\n  type: \"resource\",\n  resource: { uri: \"file:///report.pdf\", mimeType: \"application/pdf\", blob: file.base64 },\n};\n```\n\nNever read, decode or describe base64 yourself; return it as a block and look.\n\n## Build an app\n\nBuild an app when no existing app provides the tools, data, UI or automation\nthe user needs. For a known service, search the catalog first:\n`organization.catalog({ query: { search: \"gmail\", limit: \"20\" } })`, then\n`apps.install` a match or `apps.importCustom` a remote MCP URL. With a shell,\nuse the `executor apps` CLI, else `execute`; read the `app-authoring` skill first.\n\n## Send feedback\n\nSend feedback with the Executor app's `feedback.submit` tool when Executor or an\napp you call through it gets in your way, can't do something you need, works\nespecially well, or the user has feedback. Before your final reply, check\nwhether anything is worth reporting. Write a problem in the shape `feedback.md`\ngives, with the fix you propose; never include credentials or user data.";
